records.t:
the quarrel between Agamemnon and Achilles involves Agamemnon, -0749/-0724, The Iliad
from the day on which the son of Atreus, king of men,
      and great Achilles, first fell out with one another.

the quarrel between Agamemnon and Achilles involves Achilles, -0749/-0724, The Iliad
from the day on which the son of Atreus, king of men,
      and great Achilles, first fell out with one another.

Achilles is a Achaean warrior, -0749/-0724, The Iliad
I have taken
      nothing by all my hardships—with my life ever in my hand;

Ulysses is a envoy (sent to Achilles), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
We are with you as messengers
      from the host of the Danaans,

Agamemnon has taken Briseis (from Achilles), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
Agamemnon has taken her from me; he has played me
      false;

Ajax son of Telamon is a envoy (sent to Achilles), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
We are with you as messengers
      from the host of the Danaans,

envoys represent Danaans (before Achilles), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
We are with you as messengers
      from the host of the Danaans,

Sarpedon is a Lycian leader, -0749/-0724, The Iliad
Sarpedon leader of the Lycian warriors has
      fallen—he who was at once the right and might of Lycia;

Sarpedon is a Jove’s son, -0749/-0724, The Iliad
Sarpedon leader of the Lycian warriors has
      fallen—he who was at once the right and might of Lycia;

assembly contains the people (when gathered), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
Agamemnon sent the criers round to call the people in assembly;
      so they called them and the people gathered thereon.

Achilles owns horses of Achilles (immortal; inherited from Peleus), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
for they are immortal;
      Neptune gave them to my father Peleus, who in his turn gave them
      to myself;

Neptune gave horses of Achilles (to Peleus), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
Neptune gave them to my father Peleus, who in his turn gave them
      to myself;

Peleus gave horses of Achilles (to Achilles), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
Neptune gave them to my father Peleus, who in his turn gave them
      to myself;

Diomed is son of Tydeus, -0749/-0724, The Iliad
Next to
      him rose mighty Diomed son of Tydeus;

chariot races has second prize six-year-old mare (unbroken and in foal), -0749/-0724, The Iliad
For the second there was a
      six-year old mare, unbroken, and in foal to a he-ass;

Orion outrages Merope (while drunken, at Chios), -0599~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
When he was come to Chios, he outraged
Merope, the daughter of Oenopion, being drunken;

Pleiades sets Pleiades’ setting (in stormy conditions), -0599~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
and again, ‘the
stormy Peleiades go down’;

Dionysus is a heaven-born god, -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
For some say, at Dracanum; and some, on windy Icarus; and
some, in Naxos, O Heaven-born, Insewn 2502;

Dionysus is insewn Insewn, -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
For some say, at Dracanum; and some, on windy Icarus; and
some, in Naxos, O Heaven-born, Insewn 2502;

Hades is husband of Persephone (among the deathless gods), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
for I shall be no unfitting husband for you among the deathless gods,
that am own brother to father Zeus.

Persephone is wife of Hades (among the deathless gods), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
for I shall be no unfitting husband for you among the deathless gods,
that am own brother to father Zeus.

Apollo is a son of Zeus and Leto, -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
Leto’s all-glorious son goes to rocky Pytho, playing upon
his hollow lyre, clad in divine, perfumed garments;

Selene is daughter of Pallas (lord Pallas, son of Megamedes), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
while bright Selene, daughter of the lord Pallas,
Megamedes’ son

Heracles' deeds of violence is a deeds of violence (many), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
and himself did many deeds of violence and
endured many;

Heracles performs Heracles' deeds of violence (many), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
and himself did many deeds of violence and
endured many;

gods is a immortals, -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
Then all the
immortals were glad in heart and Bacchie Dionysus in especial;

Dionysus is a god (especially glad at Pan’s presentation), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
Then all the
immortals were glad in heart and Bacchie Dionysus in especial;

Pan has domain mountain peaks (including snowy crests and rocky crests), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
He has every
snowy crest and the mountain peaks and rocky crests for his domain;

Dionysus is leader of rich-haired Nymphs (in their train), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
And the Nymphs followed in his train with
him for their leader;

rich-haired Nymphs follows Dionysus (in his train), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
And the Nymphs followed in his train with
him for their leader;

Hesiod has translation George Chapman (The Georgicks of Hesiod, London, 1618), -0699~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
Of translations of Hesiod the following may be noticed:—_The Georgicks
of Hesiod_, by George Chapman, London, 1618;

Hesiod has translation Thomas Coocke (London, 1728), -0699~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
_The Works of Hesiod
translated from the Greek_, by Thomas Coocke, London, 1728;

Hesiod has translation J. Banks (translation including Hesiod, Callimachus, and Theognis), -0699~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
_The Works of Hesiod, Callimachus, and
Theognis_, by the Rev. J. Banks, M.A.;

Rudolf Rzach classifies Works and Days manuscripts (into families issuing from a common original), -0699~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
These MSS. are divided by Rzach into the following families, issuing
from a common original:

Selene has radiance (shining from her immortal head), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
From her
immortal head a radiance is shown from heaven and embraces earth;

radiance embraces earth (from heaven), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
From her
immortal head a radiance is shown from heaven and embraces earth;

Menelaus takes Helen (to the ships), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
Menelaus finds Helen and takes her to the ships, after killing
Deiphobus;

Eurytus has sons sons of Eurytus (four according to Hesiod), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
For Hesiod says Eurytus and
Antioche had as many as four sons;

Homer composes The Little Iliad (while living with Thestorides), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
While living with
Thestorides, Homer composed the _Lesser Iliad_ and the _Phocais_;

Homer composes Phocais (while living with Thestorides), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
While living with
Thestorides, Homer composed the _Lesser Iliad_ and the _Phocais_;

Homer lives with Thestorides (when composing the Lesser Iliad and the Phocais), -0699~/-0500~, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
While living with
Thestorides, Homer composed the _Lesser Iliad_ and the _Phocais_;

Athena is child of Zeus (daughter), Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica
And he said to Athena;

(ll. 174-176) ‘My daughter, will you go aid the Mice

Helios is father of seven sons of Helios (with Rhodos), -0463, The Extant Odes of Pindar
There sometime having lain with Rhodos he begat
seven sons, who had of him minds wiser than any among the men of old;

Zeus rains gold on the land, -0463, The Extant Odes of Pindar
For them Zeus brought a yellow cloud into the sky and
rained much gold upon the land;

hill of Kronos is a formerly nameless hill (when Oinomaos was king), -0475, The Extant Odes of Pindar
for theretofore it was without name, when Oinomaos was king,
and it was sprinkled with much snow[6].

Graces is required by gods (for ruling at feast or dance), -0475, The Extant Odes of Pindar
Yea even gods
without the Graces' aid rule never at feast or dance;

Aglaia enables the victory (through her gift at the Olympic games), -0475, The Extant Odes of Pindar
for that through thee, Aglaia,
in the Olympic games the Minyai's home is winner.

gods rule feast or dance (never without the Graces' aid), -0475, The Extant Odes of Pindar
Yea even gods
without the Graces' aid rule never at feast or dance;

Ixion lies with cloud phantom of Hera (mistaking it for Hera), -0469/-0467, The Extant Odes of Pindar
for he lay with a cloud, pursuing the sweet lie,
fond man

Herakles soils their bright hair (with earth), -0472, The Extant Odes of Pindar
beneath the rush of his arrows their bright hair should be
soiled with earth;

Timasarchos wins at Nemean Games (in wrestling), -0472~, The Extant Odes of Pindar
Be it the prelude of my hymn to dedicate it to Zeus the son of Kronos,
and to Nemea, and to the wrestling of Timasarchos;

Peleus is saved by Cheiron (from Akastos' ambush), -0472~, The Extant Odes of Pindar
but Cheiron delivered him
out of his hand;

Theandrid clan wins wreaths (at athletic games), -0472~, The Extant Odes of Pindar
where having put themselves to the proof they are
returning homeward, not without wreaths whose fruitage is renown;

Aiolians are brought to Tenedos (in bronze mail, with Orestes), -0445~, The Extant Odes of Pindar
for from Amyklai he came[5] with Orestes,
bringing hither an army of Aiolians in bronze mail

oracle concerning Thetis' son prevents Thetis (from marrying Zeus or his brothers), -0453~, The Extant Odes of Pindar
if she came ever into the bed of Zeus,
or of brethren of Zeus.

Fragments has unassigned portion nearly two-thirds (cannot be assigned to any distinct class), -0453~, The Extant Odes of Pindar
Nearly two-thirds of the Fragments cannot be assigned to any distinct
class:

legend of Pelops' ivory shoulder makes substitute Pelops' ivory shoulder (for Pelops' fleshly shoulder), -0471, The Extant Odes of Pindar
Pindar refuses to accept the legend which made Pelops' ivory shoulder
a substitute for his fleshly one eaten at Tantalos' table by the gods;

Pindar refuses to accept legend of Pelops' ivory shoulder, -0471, The Extant Odes of Pindar
Pindar refuses to accept the legend which made Pelops' ivory shoulder
a substitute for his fleshly one eaten at Tantalos' table by the gods;

Nausicaa is friend of Dymas’s daughter (just her own age), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
Minerva took the form of the famous sea captain Dymas’s
daughter, who was a bosom friend of Nausicaa and just her own age;

Scheria contains Phaeacian city (surrounded by a wall), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
He surrounded the city with a wall, built houses and temples,
and divided the lands among his people;

Minerva takes the form of Dymas (Dymas’s daughter and Nausicaa’s bosom friend), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
Minerva took the form of the famous sea captain Dymas’s
daughter, who was a bosom friend of Nausicaa and just her own age;

Ulysses lives in Ithaca, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
I live in Ithaca, where there is a high mountain
called Neritum, covered with forests;

Ithaca contains Neritum (a high mountain), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
I live in Ithaca, where there is a high mountain
called Neritum, covered with forests;

Telemus son of Eurymus prophesies for Cyclopes (until he grew old), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
and did all the prophesying for the Cyclopes till he
grew old;

Cyclopes receive prophecies from Telemus son of Eurymus (until he grew old), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
and did all the prophesying for the Cyclopes till he
grew old;

bull sacrifice to Neptune is made by Polyphemus, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
On this he lifted up his hands to the firmament of heaven and prayed,
saying, ‘Hear me, great Neptune;

Cyclops's curse against Ulysses is a bull sacrifice to Neptune, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
Hear me, great Neptune; if I am indeed your own true begotten
son, grant that Ulysses may never reach his home alive;

Polyphemus has eye eye, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
he turns out to be a little insignificant weakling, who has
managed to blind my eye by taking advantage of me in my drink;

Minerva plans to visit Ithaca (to put heart into Telemachus), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
In the meantime I will go to Ithaca, to
put heart into Ulysses’ son Telemachus;

Telemachus is a son of Ulysses, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
I will go to Ithaca, to
put heart into Ulysses’ son Telemachus;

the suitors abuse the suitors’ abuse of Telemachus’s household (by consuming the household’s livestock), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
the suitors of his mother
Penelope, who persist in eating up any number of his sheep and oxen;

Ulysses suffers sea hardship (while trying to save his own life and bring his men safely home), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
moreover he suffered much by sea while trying to save his
own life and bring his men safely home;

Ulysses's men perish by eating the cattle of the Sun-god Hyperion (through their own sheer folly), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
for they perished through their own sheer folly in
eating the cattle of the Sun-god Hyperion;

ship is a vessel, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
We ran our vessel upon the sands and got out of her on to the
sea shore;

ram (Cyclops’ flock) receives extra share by Ulysses's men (as an extra share for the narrator), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
As for
the ram, my companions agreed that I should have it as an extra share;

ship is loosed from her moorings, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
I then went on board and told my men to
loose the ship from her moorings;

Ulysses acquires wealth (during seven years among the Egyptians), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
I stayed there for seven years and got together much money among the
Egyptians, for they all gave me something;

Ulysses has mother slave mother (Ulysses’s mother) (a purchased slave and concubine of his father), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
whereas I was the son of a slave whom he had
purchased for a concubine;

Ulysses is husband of Penelope, -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
Madam, wife of Ulysses, Telemachus does not understand these things;
listen therefore to me

the Ithacans form the assembly (at Telemachus’s summons), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
He at once sent the criers round to call the people in
assembly, so they called them and the people gathered thereon;

the assembly is a public council of Ithaca (convened after Ulysses’s departure), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
From the day Ulysses left us
there has been no meeting of our councillors until now;

Antinous chooses twenty men (for the expedition against Telemachus), -0632~/-0599~, The Odyssey
He then chose twenty men, and they went down to their ship and to the
sea side;

Clytemnestra murders Agamemnon (by craft and entanglement), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
We slew by craft and by like craft shall die.
Swift, bring the axe that slew my lord of old;

Apollo foretells guilt of murder done should pass from me (if I slew), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
Apollo, who foretold that if I slew,
The guilt of murder done should pass from me;

The Pythian Priestess uses the sacred chair (when consulting the oracle), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
Then as a seeress to the sacred chair
I pass and sit;

the Pythian Temple at Delphi contains the sacred chair (for the priestess), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
Then as a seeress to the sacred chair
I pass and sit;

Delphi rules this their land, -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
And all this folk, and Delphos, chieftain-king
Of this their land, with honour gave him home;

Athens contains Temple of Athena (on the Acropolis), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
The scene changes to Athens. In the foreground, the Temple of Athena
on the Acropolis;

Temple of Athena is located in Athens (on the Acropolis), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
The scene changes to Athens. In the foreground, the Temple of Athena
on the Acropolis;

Hades is a realm of death (the Silent Land), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
Till to the Silent Land,
The realm of death, he cometh;

Watchman keeps watch the watch (this livelong year), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
I pray the gods to quit me of my toils,
To close the watch I keep, this livelong year;

omens at Aulis foretells Priam’s town (long the time shall be), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
_Go forth,_ he cried, _and Priam’s town shall fall.
   Yet long the time shall be;

Fate takes Fate’s hard compelling yoke, -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
Thus on his neck he took
      Fate’s hard compelling yoke;

Palace of Atreus contains a choir (within this house), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
Within this house a choir abidingly
  Chants in harsh unison the chant of ill;

Clytemnestra is a mother (of Orestes and Electra), -0457, The House of Atreus; Being the Agamemnon, the Libation-Bearers, and the Furies
Homeless we are and sold; and she who sold
Is she who bore us;

Tydeus is a Argive warrior (attacking the Proetides gate), -0466, Four Plays of Aeschylus
Tydeus already with his onset-cry
  Storms at the gate called Proetides;

Io is loved by Zeus (with passionate desire), -0429~, Four Plays of Aeschylus
Ay, thou art she whose beauty fired the breast
  Of Zeus with passion;

the daughters of Danaus are exiles from our land (unbanned by murder’s sentence), -0462~, Four Plays of Aeschylus
We flee as exiles, yet unbanned
By murder’s sentence from our land;

Chorus of the Daughters of Danaus flee the Danaids’ flight from forced marriage (because they abhor the proposed marriage), -0462~, Four Plays of Aeschylus
Let me not fall, O nevermore,
    A prey into the young men’s hand;
  Rather than wed whom I abhor,
    By pilot-stars I flee this land;

Argos is named after Apis (because Apis healed and cleansed the land), -0462~, Four Plays of Aeschylus
Styled so of old from the great healer’s name;
  For Apis, coming from Naupactus’ shore

Pelasgus is child of Palaichthon, -0462~, Four Plays of Aeschylus
I am Pelasgus, ruler of this land,
  Child of Palaichthon, whom the earth brought forth;

