Admetus entertains Heracles (in his house), -0437, Alcestis
But it so happened that
Admetus had entertained in his house the demi-god, Heracles;

Heracles is a demi-god (heroic reveller), -0437, Alcestis
But it so happened that
Admetus had entertained in his house the demi-god, Heracles;

Alcestis's funeral and burial is ordered by Admetus (with public mourning throughout Thessaly), -0437, Alcestis
Howbeit, I now will make the burial due
To this dead Queen. Be assembled, all of you;

Admetus has parent Admetus's mother (his mother), -0437, Alcestis
Word to his mother sped,
  Praying to her who bore him;

Admetus has parent Admetus's father (his father), -0437, Alcestis
Word to his father, old,
Heavy with years and cold;

Admetus loses Alcestis (as a result of her death), -0437, Alcestis
our dead is here
Made ready;

Pheres is a Thessalian (free-born and of chiefly ancestry), -0437, Alcestis
I am a Thessalian, free,
My father a born chief of Thessaly;

the Servant serves Admetus (as a household attendant), -0437, Alcestis
So here I wait in her own house, and do
Service to some black thief, some man of prey;

Pylades is betrothed to Electra (as her intended husband), -0407, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
for her,
whose marriage I have courted, I consider as my wife

marriage connects Pylades (with Electra as intended spouses), -0407, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
for her,
whose marriage I have courted, I consider as my wife

Menoeceus is descended from the Spartoi (through the dragon's jaw), -0409~, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
But thou art left among us of the race of those sown men,
pure in thy descent, both by thy mother's side and in the male line;

Parnassus is sacred to Apollo (one head), -0409~, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
The fire was on that head of Parnassus which was sacred to Apollo and
Diana;

Parnassus is sacred to Diana (one head), -0409~, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
The fire was on that head of Parnassus which was sacred to Apollo and
Diana;

Jason is a man, -0430, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
O thou abomination! thou most detested woman, both by the Gods and by
me, and by all the race of man;

Apollo is a God, -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
O mansions of Admetus, wherein I endured to acquiesce in the slave's
table,[1] though a God;

Alcestis possesses children (with Admetus), -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
But my children are enough; of these I pray the Gods
that I may have the enjoyment;

Alcestis's death occurs at Admetus's palace (inside the house), -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
who now within the
house is borne in their hands, breathing her last;

Admetus possesses Admetus's palace (as its lord), -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
O mansions of Admetus, wherein I endured to acquiesce in the slave's
table,[1] though a God;

Admetus's palace is a palace (the residence of Admetus), -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
O mansions of Admetus, wherein I endured to acquiesce in the slave's
table,[1] though a God;

Hercules rescues Alcestis (by making Death retire and taking her from the tomb), -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
and
having made Death retire, covers the lady with a robe;

Death is a priest of the dead, -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
But already I behold Death near, priest of the dead, who is about to
bear her down to the mansions of Pluto;

Death possesses Death's sword (used in rites for the dead), -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
and I am advancing upon her, that I
may begin the rites on her with my sword;

Death serves Pluto (as bearer of the dead to his realm), -0437, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
who is about to
bear her down to the mansions of Pluto;

Troy is destroyed by Greeks (by war and the spear), -0423~, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
O
Argives, ye that destroyed my city, I die willingly;

Greeks destroy Troy (in war), -0423~, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
O
Argives, ye that destroyed my city, I die willingly;

Agamemnon is a leader (with the chief authority among the Greeks), -0423~, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
But the people murmured assent, and king Agamemnon ordered the young men to
quit the virgin;

Agamemnon orders Greeks (to release Polyxena), -0423~, The Tragedies of Euripides, Volume I
